---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
const topics = [
  {id:"networks-connect-us",title:"Networks affect our lives",refs:["block-0047","block-0063"],body:"Networks connect people, organizations, and services across geographic boundaries. A network is more than the Internet: it is the infrastructure and protocols that let connected devices exchange information.",takeaway:"Network communication makes local and global collaboration possible."},
  {id:"representations",title:"Network representations and topologies",refs:["block-0002","block-0049"],body:"A topology diagram uses symbols to represent devices and their connections. A physical topology records where intermediary devices are installed and how cables are routed. A logical topology records device relationships, ports or interfaces, and the network addressing scheme. A network interface card (NIC) connects an endpoint to a network. A physical port is the connector; interface and port are often used interchangeably when discussing a device connection.",takeaway:"Physical = where equipment and cables are. Logical = how devices connect and address one another."},
  {id:"components",title:"Network components",refs:["block-0008","block-0013","block-0022","block-0039","block-0043"],body:"Hosts are end devices that participate directly in network communication. Intermediary devices connect hosts and networks. Switches, wireless access points, routers, and firewalls are examples. They may regenerate signals, maintain path information, forward traffic, and report errors. Network media carries signals: copper uses electrical impulses, fiber uses pulses of light, and wireless uses electromagnetic waves. In a client/server model, dedicated server software provides services to clients. In peer-to-peer networking, a device can act as both client and server. Peer-to-peer is inexpensive and simple, but it lacks centralized administration, is harder to secure, and does not scale well.",takeaway:"Identify whether a device is an endpoint, intermediary, or transmission medium before reasoning about its role."},
  {id:"network-types",title:"Common types of networks",refs:["block-0010","block-0019","block-0025","block-0035","block-0054"],body:"A LAN spans a limited geographic area and is commonly administered by one organization. It typically provides high-speed connectivity to local devices. A WAN spans a larger geographic area and interconnects LANs; service providers commonly operate the links, which are often slower than local LAN links. Home, SOHO, medium-to-large, and worldwide networks differ in scale. The Internet is a worldwide collection of interconnected LANs and WANs. An intranet is an organization's private network. An extranet gives authorized people outside the organization secure access to selected resources.",takeaway:"LAN describes a limited local network; WAN connects networks across larger distances. Intranet and extranet describe access boundaries."},
  {id:"internet-connections",title:"Internet connections",refs:["block-0009","block-0015","block-0038","block-0048","block-0061","block-0067","block-0071"],body:"An Internet service provider (ISP) connects customer networks to the wider Internet. Home and small-office options include cable, DSL, cellular, satellite, and dial-up. Cable and DSL are commonly always-on broadband services; cellular uses mobile networks; satellite can serve rural locations; dial-up offers low bandwidth over telephone service. Wireless Internet Service Providers (WISPs) connect subscribers to designated wireless access points or hotspots. Business connectivity may use business DSL, leased lines, or Metro Ethernet. Converged networks carry data, voice, and video over shared infrastructure and common standards.",takeaway:"Choose an access technology based on availability, required bandwidth, geography, cost, and service requirements."},
  {id:"reliable-networks",title:"Reliable network architecture",refs:["block-0018","block-0028","block-0034","block-0044","block-0053"],body:"Reliable network architecture addresses four characteristics. Fault tolerance uses redundancy and alternate paths so a failure does not necessarily stop service. Scalability allows the network to grow without degrading existing services; standards and protocols make expansion predictable. Quality of Service (QoS) manages traffic so delay-sensitive services such as voice and live video receive suitable treatment during congestion. Security protects infrastructure, devices, and transmitted information. Information security commonly targets confidentiality (only authorized parties can read data), integrity (data is not improperly altered), and availability (authorized users can access services when needed).",takeaway:"Fault tolerance, scalability, QoS, and security solve different reliability problems; they are complementary, not interchangeable."},
  {id:"trends",title:"Modern networking trends",refs:["block-0012","block-0021","block-0024","block-0031","block-0046","block-0056","block-0067"],body:"Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) lets people use personal devices for work or school. Online collaboration and video communication let distributed users work together. Cloud computing provides storage, backup, and applications through Internet-connected data centers. Public clouds serve the general public, private clouds are dedicated to an organization, hybrid clouds connect multiple cloud types, and custom clouds are designed for a particular industry's needs. Smart-home devices exchange information with appliances and services. Powerline networking carries data over electrical wiring and can help where Wi-Fi coverage or new cabling is impractical.",takeaway:"These trends increase flexibility and connectivity, but also expand the need for capacity, identity management, and security."},
  {id:"security",title:"Network security",refs:["block-0007","block-0026","block-0036","block-0042","block-0051","block-0053","block-0072"],body:"Security must be layered. Home and small-office baselines include endpoint antivirus/antispyware and firewall filtering. Larger networks may add dedicated firewalls, access control lists (ACLs), intrusion prevention systems (IPS), and virtual private networks (VPNs). External threats include malware, zero-day attacks, denial-of-service, data interception, and identity theft. Internal threats include lost or stolen devices, accidental misuse, and malicious insiders. Protect both the network infrastructure and the information carried over it. A failure that exposes patient records to other patients is a security failure, even if the network remains available.",takeaway:"Security is not one product or one setting. Use layered controls and protect confidentiality, integrity, and availability."},
  {id:"it-professional",title:"The IT professional",refs:["block-0020","block-0030","block-0050"],body:"Networking skills support roles in installation, operations, support, and network engineering. The source notes introduce Cisco Networking Academy career resources and the CCNA credential. CCNA validates foundational networking knowledge, including IP connectivity and security, wireless, virtualization, automation, and programmability. Career research should compare the responsibilities, skills, and experience expected by employers.",takeaway:"Use foundational knowledge as a platform for practical labs, troubleshooting experience, and role-specific skills."}
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Semester 1 — Networking Today — fred.fish" description="An improved lesson built from the Semester 1 networking lecture notes, with source traceability." active="cisco">
  <section class="semester-shell">
    <div class="semester-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/">← All learning modules</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="semester-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 01" label="SEMESTER 1 / SOURCE-BASED COURSE" /><h1>Networking Today.<br /><span>Understand the foundations.</span></h1><p>This course rebuilds the recovered Semester 1 lecture material into a connected set of lessons. Source references are preserved so the original notes can be audited separately from expanded explanations.</p><div class="semester-tags"><span>9 TOPICS</span><span>73 TEXT BLOCKS IN SOURCE CORPUS</span><span>IMAGE AUDIT IN PROGRESS</span></div></header>
    <section class="source-notice"><strong>Source integrity</strong><p>Each lesson lists the recovered OneNote text-block IDs it uses. Explanations are reorganized and clarified teaching material, not verbatim lecture text. Image-only notes are not yet marked complete; they will be incorporated after OCR and visual review.</p></section>
    <section class="semester-topics">
      {topics.map((topic,index)=><article class="semester-topic" id={topic.id}><div class="topic-index">{String(index+1).padStart(2,"0")}</div><div class="topic-content"><h2>{topic.title}</h2><p>{topic.body}</p><div class="topic-takeaway"><strong>Remember</strong><span>{topic.takeaway}</span></div><div class="source-refs"><span>SOURCE BLOCKS</span>{topic.refs.map((ref)=><code>{ref}</code>)}</div></div></article>)}
    </section>
    <section class="semester-footer"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Practice what you just learned.</h2><p>Start a short session. Questions will update your concept-level review schedule.</p></div><a class="semester-button" href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
<style>
.semester-shell{max-width:1050px;margin:auto;padding:45px 24px 100px}.semester-nav{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;color:var(--muted);font:11px "DM Mono",monospace}.semester-nav a:hover{color:var(--teal)}.semester-hero{padding:70px 0 34px}.semester-hero h1{font-size:clamp(42px,6vw,70px);line-height:1.03;letter-spacing:-.075em;margin:20px 0}.semester-hero h1 span{color:var(--teal)}.semester-hero p{max-width:680px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.85}.semester-tags{display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:25px;color:var(--subtle);font:9px "DM Mono",monospace;letter-spacing:.08em}.source-notice{padding:18px 20px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:3px solid var(--teal);border-radius:10px;background:var(--surface)}.source-notice p{margin:5px 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:13px}.semester-topics{margin-top:45px}.semester-topic{display:grid;grid-template-columns:55px 1fr;gap:22px;padding:30px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-soft);scroll-margin-top:30px}.topic-index{color:var(--teal);font:13px "DM Mono",monospace;padding-top:6px}.topic-content h2{font-size:25px;letter-spacing:-.045em;margin:0 0 12px}.topic-content>p{max-width:850px;color:#c0cdd1;line-height:1.85;font-size:14px}.topic-takeaway{display:grid;grid-template-columns:100px 1fr;gap:12px;padding:13px 15px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:#0c1a20;font-size:12px}.topic-takeaway strong{color:var(--teal);font:10px "DM Mono",monospace;letter-spacing:.08em;padding-top:3px}.topic-takeaway span{color:var(--text)}.source-refs{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:14px}.source-refs span{font:9px "DM Mono",monospace;color:var(--subtle);margin-right:5px}.source-refs code{font-size:10px;color:var(--muted);background:var(--surface-2);padding:3px 6px;border-radius:4px}.semester-footer{margin-top:55px;padding:28px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:15px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;background:linear-gradient(140deg,#10262b,#0c171e)}.semester-footer h2{font-size:25px;margin:8px 0}.semester-footer p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:13px}.semester-button{white-space:nowrap;background:var(--teal);color:#061513;padding:12px 16px;border-radius:8px;font-size:12px;font-weight:750}@media(max-width:640px){.semester-shell{padding:28px 18px 70px}.semester-hero{padding:48px 0 28px}.semester-topic{grid-template-columns:32px 1fr;gap:12px}.topic-content h2{font-size:21px}.topic-takeaway{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:5px}.semester-footer{align-items:flex-start;flex-direction:column}}
</style>